---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="When Should an Attorney Order a Life Care Plan? — MyLifeCarePlanning.com"
  description="Learn when to retain a life care planner for your personal injury, workers' compensation, or catastrophic injury case. Timing matters for case strategy and damages."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="flex-1">
    <section class="bg-brand-900 text-white">
      <div class="page-container py-12">
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold">When Should an Attorney Order a Life Care Plan?</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-brand-200 max-w-2xl">Timing and case selection guidance for plaintiff and defense counsel.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="page-container section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-gray">
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">A life care plan is one of the most powerful tools in a catastrophic injury case — but only when ordered at the right time and for the right type of case. Here is guidance for both plaintiff and defense attorneys.</p>

        <h2>Order a life care plan when the case involves</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Traumatic brain injury (TBI) of any severity</li>
          <li>Spinal cord injury or paralysis</li>
          <li>Amputation or limb loss</li>
          <li>Severe burns covering significant body surface area</li>
          <li>Catastrophic orthopedic injuries requiring future surgeries</li>
          <li>Permanent disability expected to last years or a lifetime</li>
          <li>Complex multi-system trauma</li>
          <li>Cases where future damages are likely to exceed $500,000</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>When to order it — timing guidance</h2>
        <p><strong>Plaintiff counsel:</strong> Order as early as possible once the client has reached maximum medical improvement (MMI) or when treating physicians have established a clear prognosis. Early ordering allows the plan to guide discovery, shape the narrative, and support demand letters and mediation.</p>
        <p><strong>Defense counsel:</strong> Order after plaintiff's plan is disclosed to evaluate and potentially rebut the scope and cost projections. Having an independent physician planner review the plaintiff's plan is increasingly standard practice in high-value cases.</p>

        <h2>Signs you need a physician life care planner specifically</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Opposing counsel has retained a physician planner</li>
          <li>The injury involves complex neurological or orthopedic conditions</li>
          <li>The plan will face Daubert or Frye challenge</li>
          <li>The case is expected to go to trial</li>
          <li>The settlement demand exceeds seven figures</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What to ask when retaining a life care planner</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>What is your medical specialty and board certification?</li>
          <li>How many life care plans have you prepared in the past year?</li>
          <li>Have you testified at deposition or trial as a life care planning expert?</li>
          <li>What is your methodology for cost projections?</li>
          <li>What is your availability for deposition and trial?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="not-prose mt-10 p-6 bg-brand-50 border border-brand-200 rounded-xl text-center">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-brand-900 mb-2">Find a Physician Life Care Planner</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Browse board-certified MDs and DOs by state.</p>
          <a href="/physician-life-care-planners" class="btn-primary text-sm">Browse by State</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
